[h1]Smart District Planner[/h1]
[b]Pick a city and the districts you want. In a few seconds it finds the layout with the highest adjacency bonus and places it as map tacks with district icons.[/b]

[b]Requires Detailed Map Tacks.[/b] It decides where a district fits and what it gets, so the numbers in a plan are the numbers on the tacks.

[h2]Why use it[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Your civilization's rules, with no setup.[/b] Adjacency and placement rules from your civilization, your leader, your pantheon and your religion's beliefs are counted, and your unique districts are used: Japan's districts get +1 from each other, Korea's Seowon loses yield next to districts, Gaul's specialty districts stay off the City Center, Vietnam's go on Woods, Rainforest or Marsh.
[*][b]Close to the best possible, and it tells you how close.[/b] With few districts it tries every layout, so the plan is the best one; otherwise it says at most how far from the best it may be, and you can press Improve. Checked against real games: from games that 8 strong players shared with their saves on CivFanatics, 17 city layouts (turns 58–276; [url=https://github.com/SmallSpider0/civ6-smart-district-planner/blob/main/docs/reference-scenes.md]each scene with maps[/url], in Chinese) were planned again on the saved maps, counting adjacency only. The players’ layouts reach on average 90.4% of the proven optimum; the Standard effort reaches 99.8% and hits it exactly in 16 of 17. The players built their cities step by step, though, without knowing what would come later.
[/list]

[img]STEAM_IMG_REF12_EN[/img]
Xi’an, turn 145 of a one-city challenge (Gathering Storm). Counting adjacency only, the player’s Holy Site, Theater Square and Campus give +12; the planner puts the Theater Square among the wonders (+6) and the Holy Site by the mountains to the west (+4), +15 in all, the proven best.

[img]STEAM_IMG_REF06_EN[/img]
Kyoto, Japan, turn 276 (Gathering Storm). Counting adjacency only, the player’s Industrial Zone, Holy Site and Theater Square give +14; with Meiji Restoration the planner puts the three in a row so they boost each other, +16. The row covers two farmed Floodplains, and counting the tiles too, the player’s layout is just as good.

[list]
[*][b]Several cities at once.[/b] Up to 8 cities, including site tacks for cities not founded yet. Districts share adjacency across cities, and districts limited to one per empire, like the Government Plaza, go to the city where they do the most. In the test scenarios, planning the cities one at a time reaches only about 95% of the best known plan.
[*][b]Borrows tiles from your other cities.[/b] Tiles you can swap from your own cities are used (on by default, can be turned off). On real saves this gives about 13% more adjacency for one city and 19% for several; some districts then need a Swap in Manage Citizens, and the result marks them.
[*][b]Never a tile you cannot build on, never moves what you have.[/b] Luxury and strategic resources stay uncovered, empire limits and city borders are checked, and on tiles a city owns the game's own list of valid tiles is checked too. Built districts and your own tacks stay where they are and count as neighbors.
[*][b]Wonders with their rules.[/b] Add wonders and they are planned with the districts: the Colosseum gets an Entertainment Complex next to it when the city has none, and the Temple of Artemis comes with its Camp.
[/list]

[h2]How to use it[/h2]
[list]
[*]In a game, click the hexagon button in the toolbar at the upper left, or press Shift+L.
[*]One city: pick a city, set the districts you must have to Must, leave the rest in the Pool, and say how many districts the city should end up with.
[*]Click Solve for three plans with different districts. Click one to preview it on the map, then Place tacks. Undo tacks works too, also after loading a save.
[*]City sites chooses spots for new cities; Several cities plans a group of cities together.
[/list]

[h2]Also[/h2]
[list]
[*]Lean toward a yield with the Science, Culture or Religion preset, or set each weight yourself.
[*]Districts stay off tiles that would make good farms or mines unless the bonus is worth it.
[*]Neighborhoods go where Appeal gives the most Housing. An Aqueduct is added when a city lacks fresh water, a Dam when it has enough Floodplains.
[*]When a plan relies on nearby Woods or Rainforest, a “Don't chop” tack on those tiles says what chopping would cost.
[*]Good places: pick a district and the map colors every tile it can go on by its bonus.
[*]To plan again, turn on “Replace this mod's earlier tacks”: the old tacks are replaced, and Undo tacks puts them back.
[*]City sites scores each site on district potential, tiles, resources and Housing; hover a score for the yields behind it. Dense packing fits in as many cities as possible.
[*]Long searches run over several frames, so you can keep playing or close the panel. One city with 6–8 districts usually takes under half a second.
[*]Settings are kept in the save.
[/list]

[h2]Questions[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Should I change the weights for my leader?[/b] Not for the rules: your civilization's, leader's, pantheon's and beliefs' bonuses are counted automatically. The weights say which yields you want and do not change by themselves; pick the Science preset if you want more Science. On Several cities, “Apply a template” fills a district list for Science, Production or another yield from your civilization's rules.
[*][b]Subscribed, but no button in the game?[/b] Enable both Detailed Map Tacks and Smart District Planner in Additional Content, then restart the game.
[*][b]Why was a district left out?[/b] The result says why: no valid tile in range, already built, empire limit reached, not available to your civilization, or not enough districts in the Pool.
[*][b]Is my save safe if I disable it?[/b] Yes. The tacks are ordinary map tacks.
[*][b]Can I use it with another planner?[/b] No. Auto Map Tacks Planner, Empire Planner and other district planners simulate, create and delete map tacks and interfere with each other. Use one per game.
[*][b]Multiplayer?[/b] Not tested.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2428969051]Detailed Map Tacks[/url]
[*]Base game, Rise and Fall, and Gathering Storm. English and Simplified Chinese, following the game language.
[*]What similar planner mods plan: a table on [url=https://github.com/SmallSpider0/civ6-smart-district-planner#similar-mods]GitHub[/url].
[/list]

How close it gets to the best plan, its speed and its known limitations: [url=https://smallspider0.github.io/civ6-smart-district-planner/en/]overview[/url]. Source code, bug reports and change log: [url=https://github.com/SmallSpider0/civ6-smart-district-planner]GitHub[/url].
